<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Gelou</h1><p class="st-meta">890 CE – 904 CE (approx.) · capital Dăbâca</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Transylvanian duchy of Gelou, called a Vlach by the Gesta Hungarorum, whose defeat opened Transylvania to the Hungarians; its existence is debated.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gesta-hungarorum">Gesta Hungarorum</a>, written around 1200, is the only source. It describes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelou">Gelou</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlachs">Vlach</a> ruling Vlachs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somes">Someș</a> valley of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-transylvania">Transylvania</a> at the time of the Hungarian conquest, with his centre usually placed at Dăbâca.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Nothing is known of the duchy beyond the chronicle, which praises the land for its salt and gold-bearing rivers and says its people were poorly armed and suffered Cuman and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pecheneg</a> raids. An early medieval fortification at Dăbâca has been excavated, but its dating is disputed.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gesta-hungarorum">Gesta</a> says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuhutum">Tuhutum</a>, a Hungarian chief, defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelou">Gelou's</a> army at the river Almaș and had him killed by the Căpuș as he fled, and the inhabitants accepted him as lord; his descendants are linked to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/realm-of-the-gyulas">Realm of the Gyulas</a>. The episode is central to debates over the early history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-transylvania">Transylvania</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-gelou.jpg" alt="Duchy of Gelou" width="320" height="427"><figcaption>Image: Țetcu Mircea Rareș · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GilauCJ2013_(4).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>890 CE – 904 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dăbâca</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gelou</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gelou's realm</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=897&amp;focus=duchy-of-gelou" aria-label="Duchy of Gelou on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5237 529 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Gelou lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5557 785L5437 760"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5437 760h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5557 785h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5557 785h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Dăbâca. <a href="/atlas?year=897&amp;focus=duchy-of-gelou">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

